# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo

# ...except the AST08 fixture corpus, which ships real bytecode on purpose.
# AST08-S08 (Bytecode Cache Poisoning) is a scenario about a .pyc that the import
# machinery selects while reviewers read the source beside it -- or with no source
# at all. A corpus that could not contain a .pyc could not encode the scenario, so
# fixtures/AST08/ is re-included here. Both lines are needed and in this order: git
# cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is still excluded.
!fixtures/AST08/**/__pycache__/
!fixtures/AST08/**/*.pyc
.venv/
venv/
.env

# Test and lint caches
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Node
node_modules/

# Tooling
.omc/

# OS
.DS_Store

# eval/skill-eval-workspace/ is DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED.
#
# This repository commits its evidence: the judge scorecards, the F1 corpus and its
# per-case verdicts are all in git, and the with/without eval workspace is the third
# kind of evidence beside them. A delta nobody can re-derive from the runs under it
# is a claim, not a measurement.
#
# The size question was asked and measured rather than assumed. One full iteration is
# ~1.4 MB of plain text across 33 cases x 2 configurations, of which ~73% is prompt.txt
# -- the exact bytes sent to the agent. prompt.txt is the largest file and the one most
# tempting to drop, and it is precisely the file that makes "the two arms differ in
# exactly one respect" auditable AFTER the run instead of only asserted before it:
# diff the two arms' prompt.txt and the only difference is the installed-skill block.
# Dropping it would save a megabyte and cost the audit.
#
# Nothing under this directory is generated by a model at a size that changes the
# answer -- responses are single-digit KB. If an iteration ever ships something large
# and genuinely re-derivable, ignore THAT FILE by name here with its own reason, and
# never the benchmark.json, grading.json, timing.json or assertion-review.json, which
# are the published artifacts.
#
# tests/test_eval_cases.py::test_the_eval_workspace_is_not_git_ignored fails if this
# decision is quietly reversed.

# Signing keys — never, under any name.
#
# scripts/sign_usf.py REFUSES to write a private key inside the working tree at all
# (the check resolves symlinks and `..` against the git toplevel, so a path that only
# looks external is refused too). These lines are the backstop for a key that arrived
# some other way: copied in by hand, dropped by another tool, or pulled from a backup.
#
# tests/test_signing_key_never_enters_the_repo.py is the alarm these lines are the
# backstop for. It fails if anything key-shaped reaches `git ls-files` OR merely sits in
# the working tree (ignored is not absent: `git add -f`, a release tarball and a backup
# all see through .gitignore), and its
# ::test_gitignore_covers_every_key_filename_suffix_the_scan_catches is parametrized over
# the scan's own suffix list, so narrowing the block below fails there rather than going
# unnoticed. This repository audits other people's supply chains; leaking its own signing
# key would be the most embarrassing possible outcome.
*.pem
*.key
*.p8
*.pkcs8
*.pkcs12
*.ppk
*.pfx
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
*.der
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
id_ecdsa*
id_dsa*
# The signing key's own directory, both segments of sign_usf.py's DEFAULT_KEY_PATH
# (~/.config/did-web/<did>/ed25519.pem). The key lives outside the repository by
# design and keygen refuses to write inside it, so these lines are the belt to
# that braces: they catch a copied key directory, a backup, or a restored tarball.
# tests/test_signing_key_never_enters_the_repo.py derives what must be here from
# DEFAULT_KEY_PATH, so moving the default without updating this fails the suite.
did-web/
jhkchan.github.io/

# Feature-workflow planning artifacts: internal working material, not a deliverable.
# Removed from history in the 2026-08-26 rewrite; keep it that way.
features/
